.TH KI 1 "2026-07-30" "Knowledge Islands" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
\&
.PP
ki \- Knowledge Islands command-line interface
.SH SYNOPSIS
\&
.SS General
\&
.TP
.B ki [command]
Run a KI command, or print root help when no command is supplied.
.SS Global options
\&
.TP
.B --help, -h
Print help for KI or the selected command.
.TP
.B --version, -V
Print the CLI version.
.SS Installation
\&
.TP
.B ki bootstrap [--refresh]
Detect agents, install the canonical harness, and install core user skills.
.SS Local management
\&
.TP
.B ki manage search <query>
Search verified installed local harness capabilities.
.TP
.B ki manage completion <shell>
Print Bash or Zsh completion source.
.TP
.B ki manage outdated
Report installed harnesses only when comparable newer release evidence is
available; it names unavailable evidence rather than claiming freshness.
.TP
.B ki manage missing
Report desired user capabilities that have no installed provider.
.TP
.B ki manage update [--cli]
Update an installer-managed CLI and refresh installed configured harnesses.
.TP
.B ki manage cleanup
Report eligible KI-managed stale state.
.TP
.B ki manage diag
Show global state and direct-CWD repository health.
.TP
.B ki manage doctor
Check user configuration, agents, harnesses, user-skill links, and direct-CWD
legacy KI state and configuration.
.TP
.B ki manage docs [topic]
Print canonical KI documentation locations.
.TP
.B ki manage list
List verified installed harnesses and capabilities with declared user skills and
local registered repositories.
.SS User management
\&
.TP
.B ki skill add <skill> [--replace]
Link a harness skill into configured user agent skill spaces.
.TP
.B ki skill remove <skill>
Remove a KI-managed user skill link.
.SS Workspace management
\&
.TP
.B ki workspace init
Create a direct-CWD \fB.ki-workspace.toml\fR.
.TP
.B ki workspace register
Discover and register nested KI workspace members.
.TP
.B ki workspace list
List configured workspace groups.
.TP
.B ki workspace show <group>
Show one workspace group.
.TP
.B ki workspace add <group> <path-or-pattern>
Add a member to one workspace group.
.TP
.B ki workspace remove <group> <path-or-pattern>
Remove a member from one workspace group.
.SS Repository options
\&
.TP
.B ki repo [--repo <path-or-pattern>]... [command]
Use repeated \fB--repo\fR options to select explicit repository roots or
patterns.
.TP
.B ki repo [--workspace <group>] [command]
Use one named group from the direct-CWD KI workspace.
.SS Repository management
\&
.TP
.B ki repo init [directory] --title <title> --description <description> --repo-code <code> --runtime <runtime>... --visibility <public|private>
Create the explicit KI identity declaration for one existing Git repository
and register its physical root locally.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] audit [--skill <skill>] [--progress <auto|always|never>] [--progress-style <single|multi>] [--reporter-levels <levels|all>]
Run registered native repository audits.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] conform [--skill <skill>] [--dry-run] [--progress <auto|always|never>] [--progress-style <single|multi>] [--reporter-levels <levels|all>]
Record the selected physical KI repository roots locally, then apply
registered native repository repairs.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] repair [--dry-run]
Record selected physical KI repository roots, then reconcile only their
proven KI-managed projections.
.TP
.B ki registry [--repo <path-or-pattern>]... [--workspace <group>] add [--dry-run]
Record selected physical KI repository roots in the local user register without
applying repository repairs.
.TP
.B ki registry list
List KI repositories registered in the local user register.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] plan list [--format <text|json>] [--horizon <horizon>] [--status <status>]
List governed work items without changing their lifecycle.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] educate [--skill <skill>]
Explain the registered repository maintenance catalogue.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] skill add <skill> [--replace]
Link and declare a harness skill in selected repositories.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] skill remove <skill>
Remove KI-managed repository skill links and declarations.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] upgrade
Refresh uniquely resolved capabilities declared by selected KI repositories.
.SS Harness management
\&
.TP
.B ki harness info <harness-id>
Inspect one installed harness and its capabilities.
.TP
.B ki harness list
List installed compatible harnesses.
.TP
.B ki harness install <harness-id>
Install one configured compatible harness.
.TP
.B ki harness reinstall <harness-id>
Replace one inactive installed harness with a verified archive.
.TP
.B ki harness uninstall <harness-id>
Remove one installed non-canonical harness.
.SS Trades
\&
.TP
.B ki trades routes add <repository> --direction export|import --kind work|knowledge
Declare one local typed directional trade route.
.TP
.B ki trades routes remove|list|check
Maintain or inspect local typed trade route declarations.
.TP
.B ki trades new|receive|list|show|release|prune
Create, pull, inspect, release, or prune cross-repository trade copies.
.SS Agora management
\&
Named profiles are read from
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/ki/agoras/<name>.ki-agora
(normally
.BR ~/.config/ki/agoras ).
.TP
.B ki agora list
List named Agora profiles.
.TP
.B ki agora show <agora>
Show one Agora profile.
.TP
.B ki agora open <agora>
Open one Agora's ordered project roster in Zed.
.SS Acquisition
\&
.TP
.B ki acquire chatgpt import <capture-directory> --output <kep-directory> [--dry-run]
Create a deterministic Knowledge Export Package from a local ChatGPT capture.
.SS Development
\&
.TP
.B ki dev local set <local-harness-path>
Validate and remember a local checkout without enabling it.
.TP
.B ki dev local on
Switch the canonical harness to the remembered local checkout.
.TP
.B ki dev local off
Restore the verified canonical harness archive.
.TP
.B ki dev skill rubric <skill> [--write]
Check or regenerate a skill's rubric catalogue.
.SH DESCRIPTION
\&
.PP
.B ki
is the Knowledge Islands command-line interface.
It provides stable, local-first commands for working with Knowledge Islands
material.
This page describes the intended V1 CLI surface.
.SH COMMAND GROUPS
\&
.PP
KI divides commands into groups by operational scope and purpose, so it is
clear whether a command affects user state, one repository, or local
development state. An asterisk marks a planned interface that is not yet
available in the installed CLI.
.SS General
\&
.PP
Use these commands to invoke KI; use \fB--help\fR for command-specific guidance.
.TP
.B ki [command]
Run a KI command, or print root help when no command is supplied.
.SS Global options
\&
.PP
Use these options with
.B ki
or any KI command to print command help or the CLI version.
.TP
.B --help, -h
Print help for KI or the selected command.
.TP
.B --version, -V
Print the CLI version.
.SS Installation
\&
.PP
Use this command to bootstrap the user-managed KI environment.
.TP
.B ki bootstrap
Create the user-managed KI environment configuration from detected supported
agents, install the built-in verified canonical harness, then install the core
user skills —
.BR ki-bootstrap ,
.BR ki-delegate ,
.BR ki-next ,
.BR ki-plan ,
and
.BR ki-recap
— into every configured agent's user skill directory. An existing agent
configuration is reused without replacement. Pass
.B --refresh
to redetect every supported runtime and rebuild the user configuration's
agents, installed harnesses, and KI-managed user skill links.
.SS Local management
\&
.PP
Use these commands to inspect the local KI installation, user configuration,
and repository state without changing it.
.TP
.B ki manage search <query>
Search only verified installed local harness capabilities. The required
non-empty query matches case-insensitively against a harness identifier,
capability kind, or capability name. Results are sorted by harness identifier,
capability kind, and capability name. The command neither discovers a
repository nor consults a registry or the network. When no capability matches,
it succeeds and reports that no matching installed capabilities exist.
.TP
.B ki manage completion <shell>
Print Bash or Zsh completion source to standard output.
.TP
.B ki manage outdated
Report installed harnesses only when comparable newer release evidence is
available; it names unavailable evidence rather than claiming freshness.
.TP
.B ki manage missing
Report desired user capabilities that have no installed provider.
.TP
.B ki manage update [--cli]
Refresh installed configured harnesses. The executable updates only when a
verified installer receipt proves that it owns the running regular executable.
Use
.B --cli
to require that executable target. Linked development and externally managed
executables, including Homebrew installations, are not self-updated.
.TP
.B ki manage cleanup
Report only stale artefacts in a persisted, versioned KI-owned format. V1 has
no such format, so this command reports that no eligible managed stale state
exists and changes no files. It does not infer that cache contents,
transaction-looking directories, unconfigured harnesses, links, or unknown
files are stale.
.TP
.B ki manage diag
Print the CLI version, installation mode, resolved paths, and user
configuration without changing state. When the current directory itself has a
regular \fB.ki-config.toml\fR, it also reports that one repository's declared
skills and compatible agent projections. It never accepts selectors, searches
ancestors, expands a workspace or mGit container, or follows a symbolic
repository declaration.
.TP
.B ki manage doctor
Check the user configuration, configured agent skill directories, installed
harnesses, and configured KI user-skill links without changing state. It also
reports direct-CWD legacy \fB.ki-meta/\fR and \fB.ki/\fR directories, without
searching for or operating on a repository. It validates a regular direct-CWD
\fB.ki-config.toml\fR declaration without resolving its providers. A failing
check produces a non-zero exit status after the complete report.
.TP
.B ki manage docs [topic]
Print canonical documentation locations without opening a browser, fetching
content, or discovering a repository. With no topic, it prints every
available location with an explanatory prefix. The supported topics are
.B overview
for
.IR https://knowledgeislands.info/tooling/cli/ ,
.B site
for
.IR https://knowledgeislands.info/ ,
.B manual
for
.IR https://github.com/knowledgeislands/tools-ki/blob/main/man/ki.1
and
.B roadmap
for
.IR https://github.com/knowledgeislands/tools-ki/blob/main/ROADMAP.md .
Any other topic is invalid command grammar.
.TP
.B ki manage list
List verified installed harnesses and their capabilities with declared user
skills and local registered repositories. The command does not fetch, discover a
repository, or change state.
.SS User management
\&
.PP
Use these commands to inspect installed capabilities and manage the skills
declared in the user-managed KI environment.
.TP
.B ki skill add <skill> [--replace]
Add a registered skill in configured user agent skill spaces.
.TP
.B ki skill remove <skill>
Remove a KI-managed user skill link.
.SS Workspace management
\&
.PP
Use these commands from a workspace directory to manage KI-owned named
repository groups without requiring the directory itself to be a KI repository.
.TP
.B ki workspace init
Create the initial workspace file with an empty \fBdefault\fR group.
.TP
.B ki workspace register
Discover the physical directory hierarchy in deterministic post-order. A
regular \fB.ki-config.toml\fR is a terminal repository leaf; symbolic links are
ignored. Create or refresh each container workspace's default group with direct
repository and nested-workspace members while preserving non-default groups.
.TP
.B ki workspace list
List every group with local and effective repository counts, marking the
default and reporting each repository's direct or nested origin.
.TP
.B ki workspace show <group>
Print the path-keyed members of one group in deterministic order.
.TP
.B ki workspace add <group> <path-or-pattern>
Add one typed repository path or pattern to a named group's members map.
A new named group is created when needed.
.TP
.B ki workspace remove <group> <path-or-pattern>
Remove one existing typed repository path or pattern from a named group map.
.SS Repository options
\&
.PP
Use this shared option with any
.B ki repo
command to select one or more repositories. It appears after
.B ki repo
and before the command.
.TP
.B --repo <path-or-pattern>
Repeat this option to select literal repository roots or patterns in supplied
order. KI resolves every target physically before an operation begins and
rejects unmatched patterns, invalid roots, and duplicate physical roots.
.TP
.B --workspace <group>
Select one named group from a regular direct-CWD \fB.ki-workspace.toml\fR.
This option and \fB--repo\fR are mutually exclusive. Workspace paths and
patterns resolve from that file's physical directory. With neither selector,
KI uses the workspace default group when that direct-CWD file exists, before
checking a regular direct-CWD \fB.mgit-config.toml\fR. KI follows standard
members, nested members' \fBmain/\fR checkouts, and \fBdir\fR containers; it
ignores mGit symlinks and bare stores. It never searches ancestor directories
for either configuration or invokes \fBmgit\fR. Without a direct-CWD
configuration, KI discovers one repository from the current working directory.
.SS Repository management
\&
.PP
Use these commands to inspect and maintain declared capabilities and manage
their skill links. Read-only operations isolate per-target diagnostics; a later
mutation failure retains earlier successful targets and returns a non-zero
result.
.TP
.B ki repo init [directory] --title <title> --description <description> --repo-code <code> --runtime <runtime>... --visibility <public|private>
Initialize exactly one existing physical Git worktree root. With no
\fIdirectory\fR, it uses the current directory; an explicit directory must be
that worktree's root and must not be a symbolic link. Every identity value is
required: \fB--title\fR, \fB--description\fR, a stable uppercase
\fB--repo-code\fR, one or more \fB--runtime\fR values selected from
\fBclaude-code\fR and \fBcodex\fR, and \fB--visibility public|private\fR.
It atomically creates the minimal canonical \fBki-repo\fR declaration and
registers the physical root in the local user configuration. It never runs
\fBgit init\fR, guesses identity, activates repository skills, creates a
workspace, accepts repository selectors, or overwrites an existing
\fB.ki-config.toml\fR.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] audit [--skill <skill>] [--progress <auto|always|never>] [--progress-style <single|multi>] [--reporter-levels <levels|all>]
Run the native read-only operations registered by each selected repository's
declared capabilities. The host verifies every selected harness inventory and
integrity-covered operation before importing it in process; it never dispatches
repository-vendored runners.
Progress defaults to \fBauto\fR, which follows stderr's TTY state; \fBalways\fR
also renders deterministic progress in redirected output, and \fBnever\fR
suppresses it.
Use \fB--progress-style multi\fR for stable per-skill rows.
Each per-skill result names the assessed repository first and its supplying
harness skill in brackets. Audit renders FAIL and WARN findings by default, but
always shows a terminal per-skill result so a clean assessment is visible;
\fB--reporter-levels\fR accepts a case-insensitive comma-separated selection
from FAIL, WARN, FIXED, INFO, NOT_APPLICABLE, and PASS, or \fBall\fR.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] conform [--skill <skill>] [--dry-run] [--progress <auto|always|never>] [--progress-style <single|multi>] [--reporter-levels <levels|all>]
Stage the same capabilities' safe writes, then apply them and re-audit. All
selected initial audits must pass before the staged conform writes are
published: a blocking finding aborts before publication, leaving every proposed
conform write unapplied. Output labels proposed writes separately from writes
applied after that gate; a dry run validates the complete staged write set and
reports writes it would apply without changing the repository. This boundary
does not include independent local registry updates; it also excludes later
selected repositories, subprocess conforms, and rollback after publication has
started. Publication refuses unsafe, changed, duplicate, or symbolic-link
targets.
Conform uses the same progress controls and reporter-level vocabulary as audit,
names both the target and supplier for every skill, and renders FAIL, WARN, and
FIXED findings by default while retaining every terminal per-skill result.
For a local invocation it records each selected physical root in the user
registry before resolving declarations or evaluating findings. Registration is
an inventory action, not evidence of conformance, so a failing repository
remains available for later repair and bulk maintenance.
.TP
.B ki registry [--repo <path-or-pattern>]... [--workspace <group>] add [--dry-run]
Record selected physical KI repository roots in the local user configuration
without resolving their declarations or applying repository repairs. This is an
explicit inventory action: it validates only that every selected target is a
physical directory with a regular \fB.ki-config.toml\fR and preserves existing
registry entries. It never searches outside the supplied target selection.
.TP
.B ki registry list
List physical KI repository roots explicitly registered in the local user
configuration, one absolute path per line. The command neither discovers
repositories nor changes state.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] plan list [--format <text|json>] [--horizon <horizon>] [--status <status>]
List canonical governed work items from every selected repository without
changing their lifecycle. The default text output groups ordered items by
repository; \fB--format json\fR emits the same fields in one stable object.
\fB--horizon\fR
and \fB--status\fR filter before rendering. A missing roadmap directory or
malformed or unsafe item is an isolated diagnostic for that repository; other
selected repositories still report. The command never creates, transitions,
accepts, prunes, or repairs a work item.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] educate [--skill <skill>]
Explain each selected repository's declared capabilities and their recommended
maintenance actions without changing repository state.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] repair [--dry-run]
Record each selected physical repository root in the local user register, then
reconcile only missing, dangling, or stale KI-managed compatible projections.
It never creates a declaration, adds an undeclared skill, overwrites foreign
content, guesses a provider or runtime, or follows a symbolic declaration.
\fB--dry-run\fR reports the same actions without changing disk state.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] skill add <skill> [--replace]
Add a registered skill in each selected repository.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] skill remove <skill>
Remove a KI-managed repository skill link and declaration from each selected
repository.
.TP
.B ki repo [repo-options] upgrade
Refresh uniquely resolved harness providers declared by each selected KI
repository. It changes neither user nor repository skill activation.
.SS Harness management
\&
.PP
Use these commands to install, inspect, and remove compatible harness payloads
in the user-managed KI environment.
.TP
.B ki harness info <harness-id>
Inspect one user-installed compatible harness and its capability
inventory without changing user state.
.TP
.B ki harness list
List compatible harnesses already installed.
.TP
.B ki harness install \fIharness-id\fR
Install a harness from which capabilities, such as skills and subagents, can
be added.
.TP
.B ki harness reinstall <harness-id>
Replace one inactive installed harness with a verified archive. The command
refuses a harness that supplies active user skills. The canonical harness must
first be restored with
.B ki dev local off
when it is development-linked.
.TP
.B ki harness uninstall <harness-id>
Remove one non-canonical harness with only recognised payload directories. The
canonical harness cannot be removed, and a harness that supplies active user
skills cannot be removed. A successful removal deletes its identifier from
.BR [harnesses].ids .
.SS Trades
\&
.PP
Use these commands with repositories that declare the portable
\fBki-trades\fR capability and are present in the local registered repository
estate. They preserve repository authority: every mutation writes only the
current repository, a typed reciprocal route grants visibility rather than
acceptance or retention, and no command changes a peer roadmap or knowledge.
.TP
.B ki trades routes add <repository> --direction export|import --kind work|knowledge
Declare one local typed route to a canonical HTTPS GitHub repository. Repository
identity remains the required \fBrepository\fR value in the ki-repo table.
.TP
.B ki trades routes remove <repository> --direction export|import --kind work|knowledge
Remove one local typed route.
.TP
.B ki trades routes list
List local typed routes with their registered-estate state.
.TP
.B ki trades routes check [repository] [--direction export|import] [--kind work|knowledge]
Check declared routes for reciprocal registered-repository evidence by kind.
.TP
.B ki trades new --to <repository> --kind work|knowledge --title <title> --source-ref <reference> --context <text> --submission <text> --constraints <text>
Create one outbound typed trade in the current repository.
.TP
.B ki trades receive --from <repository> --kind work|knowledge [--id <trade-id>]
Pull eligible outbound trades of one kind into the current repository as inbound
copies with status \fBreceived\fR.
.TP
.B ki trades list [--direction inbound|outbound] [--status <status>] [--kind work|knowledge] [--repo <repository>]
Read visible trade copies in the registered estate.
.TP
.B ki trades show <trade-id>
Show every visible copy of a trade.
.TP
.B ki trades release <trade-id>
Remove the current repository's outbound copy only after observing a terminal
receiver disposition.
.TP
.B ki trades prune <trade-id>
Remove the current repository's terminal inbound copy only after sender release
is observable.
.SS Acquisition
\&
.PP
Use this command to create a deterministic local Knowledge Export Package
without contacting external services.
.TP
.B ki acquire chatgpt import <capture-directory> --output <kep-directory> [--dry-run]
Validate a user-prepared local ChatGPT capture and create a deterministic
Knowledge Export Package (KEP).
The command does not control a browser, contact ChatGPT, read credentials,
discover a repository, or extract reusable knowledge.
.SS Development
\&
.PP
Use these commands to switch the canonical harness between its verified archive
and a local development checkout.
.TP
.B ki dev local set <local-harness-path>
Validate and remember a local checkout without changing the active canonical
payload or managed user-skill links. The checkout
must contain the core bootstrap skill at
.IR skills/keystone/ki-bootstrap/SKILL.md
and physical
.IR skills ,
.IR subagents ,
and
.IR hooks
payload directories.
.TP
.B ki dev local on
Enable the remembered local checkout for development. KI replaces only the
canonical harness's managed payload directories with links to that checkout,
records the local path in
.BR [local].path ,
and reconciles all eight managed core user-skill links to that source on every
run.
.TP
.B ki dev local off
Remove that recognised local payload projection, reinstall the verified
canonical archive, retain
.BR [local].path ,
and reconcile all eight managed core user-skill links to the restored archive.
.TP
.B ki dev skill rubric <skill> [--write]
Check or regenerate a skill's rubric catalogue.
.SH PATHS
\&
.PP
Each KI path uses the first non-empty value:
.nf
$KI_DATA_HOME   | $XDG_DATA_HOME/ki   | ~/.local/share/ki
$KI_CONFIG_HOME | $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/ki | ~/.config/ki
$KI_CACHE_HOME  | $XDG_CACHE_HOME/ki  | ~/.cache/ki
$KI_STATE_HOME  | $XDG_STATE_HOME/ki  | ~/.local/state/ki
.fi
Run
.B ki manage diag
to inspect the effective values.
.SH FILES
\&
.TP
.I .ki-workspace.toml
Optional schema-1 workspace manifest in a non-repository container directory.
It defines named groups of path-keyed repository and nested-workspace members.
.PP
The manifest has a required default group and a typed \fBmembers\fR map. Each
relative path key names a table containing \fBkind\fR, either
\fBrepository\fR or \fBworkspace\fR. Repository path keys may contain
patterns; workspace path keys may not. Nested workspace members recursively
expand their default groups. Other schema versions and historical array or flat
\fBrepositories\fR group forms are rejected.
.TP
.I .ki-config.toml
Required repository configuration at a KI repository root.
It declares the repository identity, metadata, and provided skills.
.SH DEVELOPMENT INSTALLATIONS
\&
.PP
After the first verified release, the Homebrew tap will install the same regular
release executable.
For a direct verified install, download
.B install.sh
from an exact KI release tag and run it with that tag, for example
.BR "bash install.sh v1.2.3" .
The installer verifies the Ed25519-signed checksum manifest before it downloads
and installs the target archive. It supports Apple Silicon and Intel macOS, and
x86_64 glibc Linux.
For development against this checkout, run
.B ./install.sh --link
to place a symbolic link in
.B KI_CLI_INSTALL_DIR
(default
.IR ~/.local/bin ).
The linked command executes the current TypeScript source through Bun without
changing its release version, and links the manual into
.B KI_MAN_INSTALL_DIR
(default: the corresponding
.IR share/man/man1
directory).
Use a dedicated development command directory or invoke
.B ./bin/ki
directly when comparing it with Homebrew's installed executable. The linked
source mode requires Bun; a verified release executable does not.
.SH EXIT STATUS
\&
.TP
.B 0
The command completed successfully.
.TP
.B 1
An operational check or requested operation failed.
.TP
.B 2
The command grammar was invalid.
.SH SEE ALSO
\&
.PP
The project README and the Knowledge Islands public tooling guide.
